      <div class="section-kicker"><span>03.02</span> Correction record guide</div>
      <h1>Correct the record.<br /><em>Do not overclaim control.</em></h1>
      <p class="content-lede">When a public statement becomes incomplete, inaccurate, outdated, or mislabeled, the useful response is a record a reader can inspect: the exact claim, relevant source, context, accountable owner, and dated resolution. That is more useful than silent revision or unsupported denial.</p>
      <aside class="content-block evidence-frame" aria-label="Framework boundary"><p class="eyebrow eyebrow--vermillion">Framework boundary</p><p>This is a Coreweaver Field Index <strong>proposed correction-record framework</strong>. It does not force a publisher, search engine, model, platform, court, or other third party to alter an external record. It is not a legal remedy, reputation-recovery service, truth-verification system, or guarantee of a commercial outcome.</p></aside>
      <section class="content-block"><h2>Start from the record, not the desired result</h2><p>A correction request becomes reviewable when it points to a specific, public statement and explains the narrow change requested. “Remove this” is not enough on its own. A usable request identifies the original wording, the date or version, the missing context or error, and the source that bears on the request.</p><p>That distinction matters when information is passed through pages, citations, summaries, search results, and conversations. A source-backed correction can improve the public record available to readers. It cannot promise a particular downstream summary, ranking, model response, legal conclusion, or buyer decision.</p></section>
      <section class="content-block"><h2>The six-part correction record</h2><div class="content-table-wrap"><table class="content-table"><thead><tr><th>Record component</th><th>What to retain</th></tr></thead><tbody>{recordFields.map(([title, description]) => <tr><td>{title}</td><td>{description}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></section>
      <section class="content-block"><h2>What a provenance record can—and cannot—do</h2><p>C2PA describes Content Credentials as an open standard for establishing the origin and edits of digital content. That history can give a reader useful context about an asset. It does not by itself establish whether every statement associated with the asset is accurate, complete, lawful, or accepted by another system.[1]</p><p>For a public correction, provenance is one possible evidence component—not a substitute for an attributable source, a clear statement of what changed, or a responsible reviewer.</p></section>
      <section class="content-block"><h2>Keep responsibility visible</h2><p>NIST describes the AI Risk Management Framework as a voluntary resource for incorporating trustworthiness considerations into the design, development, use, and evaluation of AI systems. Its governance framing is useful here as a prompt: a correction process should make ownership, review, and limitations visible. It does not make this guide a compliance program or validate any particular information system.[2]</p><p>Editorial correction guidance similarly emphasizes prompt, transparent corrections, a clear explanation of the change, and an accessible route for readers to raise possible errors. These practices inform the framework; they are not borrowed authority for a non-journalistic public record.[3]</p></section>
      <section class="content-block"><h2>When the correct answer is “not resolved”</h2><p>Some records remain contested, incomplete, private, or unsupported by the available material. In those cases, label the status honestly. A responsible record can say that a correction was requested, the evidence was insufficient, or the review is pending. Do not silently erase material history, fabricate support, or imply that a disagreement has been settled.</p><p>When a request concerns legal, medical, financial, safety, security, privacy, or another high-stakes matter, the public record should be reviewed by the people accountable for that domain before any substantive claim is changed.</p></section>
      <section class="content-block"><h2>Use a correction record to improve the next decision</h2><p>A correction path should make the next action clearer: update an attributable statement, add a caveat, request stronger evidence, retain the current statement with an explanation, or route the issue to a responsible reviewer. The goal is not to manufacture a favorable narrative. It is to make the record more legible to a reader deciding what to trust or investigate next.</p><p><a class="button" href="/field-index/evidence-review-worksheet">Open the evidence review worksheet <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p></section>
      <section class="content-block"><h2>Sources and limits</h2><p>The six-part correction record is a Coreweaver Field Index proposal. C2PA, NIST, and the Ethics and Journalism Initiative sources below provide limited context on provenance, governance, and transparent corrections. They do not certify a person, organization, asset, system, correction, or outcome.</p><ol><li><a href="https://c2pa.org/" rel="external">Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity, “Advancing digital content transparency and authenticity.”</a> Checked 2026-08-27.</li><li><a href="https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework" rel="external">National Institute of Standards and Technology, “AI Risk Management Framework.”</a> Checked 2026-08-27.</li><li><a href="https://ethicsandjournalism.org/resources/best-practices/best-practices-corrections/" rel="external">Ethics and Journalism Initiative, “Best Practices: Corrections.”</a> Checked 2026-08-27.</li></ol><p><a class="text-link" href="/field-index/editorial-policy">Read the editorial policy <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p></section>
      <section class="corrections" aria-labelledby="correction-request-title"><div class="corrections__frame evidence-frame"><div><p class="eyebrow eyebrow--vermillion">Correction pathway</p><h2 id="correction-request-title">Found a material <em>record problem?</em></h2></div><div><p>Send the public record URL or identifier, the suggested correction, and the public source that supports it. Coreweaver reviews material correction requests before adding a dated change note.</p><a class="button" href="mailto:hello@coreweaverlabs.com?subject=Field%20Index%20correction%20record">Submit a correction <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section>
